                <pb facs="0570" n="508" sameAs="frus:frus1878/pg508"/>

                <head>No. 316.<lb/><hi rendition="#italic">Mr. Bingham to Mr. Evarts</hi>.</head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Legation of the United States</hi>,<lb/><hi rendition="#italic"
                                >Tokei, Japan</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1878-09-12"><hi rendition="#italic">September</hi> 12,
                        1878</date>. (Received October 18.)</dateline>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">No. 855.]</seg></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: On the 23d ultimo, I regret to say, there
                    was a revolt among the Imperial troops in this capital, in which the second
                    battalion of the artillery of the Imperial Guard was actively engaged, beginning
                    the revolt near midnight by firing signal guns from their barracks and moving
                    from their quarters into the streets, where they met resistance from the
                    Imperial forces, and after some hours of flight and pursuit, and some
                    skirmishing and loss of life, the insurgents surrendered.</p>
                <p>It appears that by concert it had been agreed upon between this battalion and the
                    artillery of the Tokei garrison that the latter should, upon the firing of the
                    signal-guns, join in the uprising, but the war department having been secretly
                    advised of the proposed revolt had on that day moved the garrison some few miles
                    from Tokei, and thus prevented its co-operation in the conflict. Much has been
                    said of this affair, and no doubt the reported disaffection among the troops in
                    different parts of the empire has been greatly exaggerated.</p>
                <p>I inclose for your information the comments of the Japan Herald upon the affair,
                    together with the various notifications of his excellency the prime minister and
                    the minister of war, &amp;c., in relation to the revolt.</p>
                <p>All is quiet now in the capital.</p>
                <p>I have, &amp;c.,</p>
                <closer><signed>JNO. A. BINGHAM.</signed></closer>





